# Everything under content/ is per-host data: a user's own drafts and
# dossiers, the ledger, the bib export, their acronym vocabulary
# (content/acronyms.toml -- assets/style/acronyms.toml.example is the
# template, assets/style/README.md the shape), and every cache the
# pipeline builds.
#
# One blanket rule rather than a line per directory. The enumerated form
# this replaces went stale the moment a new artefact directory landed --
# content/specs/ in 5.34.0 -- and nothing failed: the new directory
# simply started showing up as untracked in every `git status`, which is
# a defect nobody notices until they run `git add -A`.
content/*

# Two exceptions, tracked deliberately: one worked draft of each genre
# and the renders that match it, so a fresh clone can see what this
# pipeline produces before it has a corpus of its own. Re-including a
# nested path means re-including its parents first -- git never descends
# into an ignored directory, so a bare `!content/drafts/<topic>/` would
# never be reached.
!content/drafts/
content/drafts/*
!content/drafts/digital-twins-for-software-engineers/
!content/rendered/
content/rendered/*
!content/rendered/digital-twins-for-software-engineers/

# ...with one carve-out from those exceptions: an evidence sidecar
# (chitragupta/evidence_appendix.py) is never tracked, in the example
# topic or anywhere else. It exists to carry verbatim quoted spans from
# the sources a draft cites, which is exactly why content/dossiers/ is
# ignored above -- and the two directories re-included here are the only
# places under content/ where a generated file could otherwise reach a
# public commit. Last matching rule wins in git, so this line has to
# follow the re-inclusions rather than precede them.
#
# The `.evidence` infix is `evidence_appendix.SUFFIX`, and
# tests/test_evidence_appendix.py asserts git ignores the path that
# module actually chooses rather than a copy of this pattern -- so
# renaming the convention fails a test instead of quietly publishing a
# quote.
content/rendered/**/*.evidence.*

# The pipeline's rotated log file (see config.toml's [logging]
# section) -- written by python -m chitragupta.corpus sync and scripts/enrich.py alike,
# per-host, and the whole directory rather than *.log below,
# since a rotated backup (pipeline.log.1, pipeline.log.2, ...) doesn't end in
# .log.
logs/


__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
# No trailing slash: the venv is a directory on a normal checkout, but a
# *symlink* to a shared one in a git worktree, and `.venv-full/` matches
# only the directory -- so the symlink shows up as an addable file.
.venv-full
venv/
env/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
# coverage's parallel mode writes one .coverage.<host>.<pid>.<random>
# file per process instead of the single .coverage above, so a plain
# `.coverage` entry doesn't catch them and they land as untracked junk
# a `git add -A` will happily commit.
.coverage.*
# `--cov-report=xml`, which .github/workflows/ci.yml passes so Codecov has
# something to upload. Not caught by either entry above: the file is
# `coverage.xml`, with no leading dot, so `.coverage.*` misses it. Anyone
# reproducing CI's exact pytest invocation locally generates one.
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
*.egg-info/
*.log
*.tmp
*.swp
.DS_Store
release/
dist/
# Generated by scripts/release.py -- README.md rewritten with absolute
# GitHub URLs, for pyproject.toml's `readme` (PyPI's renderer has no
# relative-link base to resolve against, unlike GitHub and the mkdocs
# site). Regenerated on every release build; never hand-edited.
README.pypi.md
# papers/bibliography.bib is a manual, per-host export from a personal
# reference manager (see AGENTS.md) -- not shippable source, so it's
# gitignored rather than tracked. papers/ also holds that export's
# companion attachment folder, likewise per-host.
papers/

# bench/ work lists are generated by bench/make_corpus.py and carry
# absolute paths into papers/, so they're per-host data for the same
# reason the bib file above is. bench/results/*.jsonl is deliberately NOT
# ignored: those are citekeys and durations, no paths, and they're the
# evidence bench/RESULTS.md rests on.
bench/corpus.json
bench/sample*.json
bench/par_*/

# Same "no draft/source prose in a committed result" discipline as
# bench_overlap_embed.py's KEPT_FIELDS: pairs.json (bench_paraphrase_hunt.py),
# ground_truth.json (bench_retrieval_ground_truth.py) and candidates.md
# (bench_claim_support.py) all carry claim text (and, for candidates.md, a
# matched source excerpt) quoted straight out of the drafted book, so none
# of the three is committed -- only labels.json (judgments, no claim text)
# is. Each script's docstring says how to regenerate its file locally.
bench/results/*/pairs.json
bench/results/*/ground_truth.json
bench/results/*/candidates.md

# bench_retrieval_compare.py's own subprocess-worker payloads
# (_dense_worker_<model>.json, _cascade_worker.json) are scratch --
# comparison.json already carries every number they fed into it -- and
# embed_models.py's specter2_paper_cache.json is a 10MB+ per-host SPECTER2
# vector cache with no invalidation key (see embed_paper()'s own
# docstring), not evidence to keep. None of the three should ever land
# in a `git add bench/results/<tag>`.
bench/results/*/_*_worker*.json
bench/results/specter2_paper_cache.json

# config.toml is per-host: every user edits it (parser backend, paths,
# worker count) and those edits are nobody else's defaults. The tracked
# file is config.toml.example; chitragupta/config.py fails with a copy-this
# message when config.toml is absent, rather than silently falling back,
# so a host is never running settings its owner didn't choose.
config.toml
# ...except the sample project's, which is a shipped input of the
# worked examples, not per-host data (committed with `git add -f`).
!examples/sample-project/config.toml

# Built documentation site (mkdocs.yml -> .github/workflows/docs.yml).
# Generated from the repository itself; nothing here is a source file.
site/
